WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.435 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: This time,
 Dr. Michelle Oakley</i>

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.270 line:0%
<i> kicks it into high gear.</i>

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.538 line:0%
MICHELLE: Nice shot!

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:07.407 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: As she squares off
 with a big pig...</i>

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:08.641 line:0%
MICHELLE: No!

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:11.077 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: To curb its appetite
 for destruction.</i>

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:13.646 line:0%
MICHELLE: My husband is
wrestling a boar.

00:00:13.646 --> 00:00:18.184 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: And a herd of caribou
 face a 1,600-mile journey home,</i>

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.486 line:0%
<i> but when one passenger
 suffers a rare reaction...</i>

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:22.054 line:0%
MICHELLE: She's kind of dragging
her head and pawing,

00:00:22.054 --> 00:00:23.489 line:0%
like, frantically.

00:00:23.489 --> 00:00:26.392 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The team scrambles
 to stage a roadside rescue.</i>

00:00:26.392 --> 00:00:27.660 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, yeah,
you're looking rough.

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:37.436 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 After a brutal winter,</i>

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:41.440 line:0%
<i> Haines, Alaska, is
 coming back to life.</i>

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.543 line:0%
(bellowing)

00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:46.312 line:0%
<i> The coastal town
 brims with creatures</i>

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:51.251 line:0%
<i> out to meet their mates
 and grab some grub.</i>

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:58.124 line:0%
<i> But for the area's
 canine residents,</i>

00:00:58.124 --> 00:01:03.329 line:0%
<i> springtime is high time
 for a killer disease.</i>

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.865 line:0%
<i> An outbreak of parvo virus
 is starting to spread</i>

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:07.533 line:0%
<i> among the local pet population.</i>

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.168 line:0%
MICHELLE: Hey, Sophie!

00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:11.737 line:0%
You check out that and I'm gonna
see how quick I can pinch you.

00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:14.774 line:0%
In the spring in these parts
of Alaska and the Yukon,

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.675 line:0%
parvo outbreaks
happen every year,

00:01:16.675 --> 00:01:18.878 line:0%
and we've got one just
amping up right now.

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:20.746 line:0%
What the heck,
that was fast, hey?

00:01:20.746 --> 00:01:22.215 line:0%
Next!

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:24.283 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: At the American
 Bald Eagle Foundation,</i>

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:26.852 line:0%
<i> Dr. Michelle Oakley holds
 a vaccination clinic</i>

00:01:26.852 --> 00:01:28.321 line:0%
<i> to keep the virus
 from spreading.</i>

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:29.355 line:0%
MICHELLE: Come on, Gus!
(kissing)

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:31.557 line:0%
Come here!

00:01:31.557 --> 00:01:33.592 line:0%
All the dogs are getting out
and visiting with each other,

00:01:33.592 --> 00:01:35.695 line:0%
and so parvo just takes off.

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:38.464 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Parvo is passed
 through a dog's stool.</i>

00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:42.768 line:0%
<i> It causes fever, dehydration,
 and internal hemorrhaging.</i>

00:01:42.768 --> 00:01:46.272 line:0%
MICHELLE: Parvo can kill them,
you know, kind of overnight,

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:48.807 line:0%
so we're like, okay, we better
get this, everybody vaccinated

00:01:48.807 --> 00:01:49.842 line:0%
as fast as we can.

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:51.410 line:0%
Pinch. There! All done!

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:52.444 line:0%
TRACY: Good girl.

00:01:52.444 --> 00:01:53.612 line:0%
MICHELLE: What's the dog's name?
Mutt?

00:01:53.612 --> 00:01:55.748 line:0%
TRACY: Um, no.
SIERRA: No.

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:57.349 line:0%
MICHELLE: That's what it says.
SIERRA: The breed is mutt.

00:01:57.349 --> 00:01:58.918 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh.
(laughs)

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.453 line:0%
SIERRA: Okay. Is she spayed?

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.221 line:0%
WOMAN: No, she's not.
SIERRA: No? Okay.

00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:04.190 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Today Michelle's
 daughter Sierra</i>

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.226 line:0%
<i> and vet tech Tracy are on board
 to help control the chaos.</i>

00:02:07.226 --> 00:02:08.261 line:0%
(barking)

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:09.695 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, drama!

00:02:09.695 --> 00:02:12.732 line:0%
It's starting to get
a little crazy in here.

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:14.700 line:0%
Oh, oh, puppy breath!

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.602 line:0%
These vaccine clinics
are so important,

00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:18.237 line:0%
because I'm not here
all the time,

00:02:18.237 --> 00:02:20.473 line:0%
so they can get that done and
they don't have to go, you know,

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:23.375 line:0%
a ten-hour drive or a ferry
to get care for their pets.

00:02:23.375 --> 00:02:24.744 line:0%
TRACY: Way to go!

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:26.812 line:0%
MICHELLE: When you do come,
people are just so thankful.

00:02:26.812 --> 00:02:28.881 line:0%
'Thank you for coming here,
we were so concerned.'

00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:32.852 line:0%
Ha ha, they're all done.
You probably didn't even notice.

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:34.253 line:0%
They didn't even, yeah.

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:35.454 line:0%
MAN: Whoa!

00:02:35.454 --> 00:02:37.756 line:0%
MICHELLE: So, it's,
it worked out great today.

00:02:37.756 --> 00:02:40.592 line:0%
I think my finger went right
into his back door on that one.

00:02:40.592 --> 00:02:41.828 line:0%
Just remind me to wash my hand.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:51.704 line:0%
(music)

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:53.505 line:0%
Oh, look,
there's deer right there.

00:02:53.505 --> 00:02:55.441 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: With one crisis
 under control,</i>

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:56.875 line:0%
<i> Michelle and
 her daughter Willow</i>

00:02:56.875 --> 00:03:00.313 line:0%
<i> head into a winter wonderland
 to tackle another.</i>

00:03:01.214 --> 00:03:02.481 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah.

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:05.385 line:0%
Well, we just passed the sign
for Moose Mountain.

00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:07.386 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: In faraway
 Saskatchewan,</i>

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:09.422 line:0%
<i> cold weather's
 still hanging on.</i>

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:10.957 line:0%
MICHELLE:
We're in Moose Mountain,

00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:13.893 line:0%
and we're up here
to catch wild boar.

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.428 line:0%
(snorting)

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:16.629 line:0%
The concern with wild boar

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:19.765 line:0%
is that they are actually
fairly destructive.

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:21.600 line:0%
They're an invasive species.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.737 line:0%
They'll eat young deer,
tons of frogs,

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:27.406 line:0%
and there's a lot of concern
for some ground nesting birds.

00:03:27.406 --> 00:03:28.507 line:0%
(squeal)

00:03:28.507 --> 00:03:30.510 line:0%
They're quite large,
they're quite nasty,

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.445 line:0%
so they need to do
something about it.

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:38.384 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Today, Michelle is
 meeting Dr. Ryan Brook</i>

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:40.419 line:0%
<i> of the University
 of Saskatchewan</i>

00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:42.621 line:0%
<i> to help stop
 the rampaging boars.</i>

00:03:42.621 --> 00:03:44.691 line:0%
MICHELLE: Hi!
DR. BROOK: Hey, how are you?

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:46.692 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Dr. Brook leads
 a research project</i>

00:03:46.692 --> 00:03:49.595 line:0%
<i> to collar and track
 the nuisance animals.</i>

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:50.797 line:0%
DR. BROOK: Our biggest challenge
with wild pigs

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:52.865 line:0%
is this really high
reproductive rate.

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:54.633 line:0%
These animals are having
one, two,

00:03:54.633 --> 00:03:56.936 line:0%
almost three litters per year.

00:03:56.936 --> 00:03:58.805 line:0%
They can spread disease
to humans,

00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:00.973 line:0%
pets, wildlife, livestock.

00:04:00.973 --> 00:04:02.976 line:0%
So the question is,
well, what do we do?

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:04.610 line:0%
WILLOW: Hi, Dad!

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.680 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Today's field work
 also entails a family reunion.</i>

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.616 line:0%
SHANE: Oh, getting big.

00:04:09.616 --> 00:04:10.883 line:0%
Look at that.

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:13.586 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Willow's dad has
 joined the pig patrol.</i>

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:16.355 line:0%
SHANE: You miss me?
WILLOW: Yeah.

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:18.357 line:0%
MICHELLE: Shane and I
both travel for work.

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:19.758 line:0%
In the summer,
he's a firefighter.

00:04:19.758 --> 00:04:21.460 line:0%
In the winter,
he does wildlife captures.

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.996 line:0%
And so, he's out
on the wild boar work,

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:27.866 line:0%
he's the net gunner doing the
actual captures and collaring.

00:04:27.866 --> 00:04:29.835 line:0%
SHANE: Yeah, I'd like
to put that on you.

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:31.870 line:0%
Know where you are at all times.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:33.473 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: While Shane
 and Willow catch up,</i>

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:35.574 line:0%
<i> Michelle gets
 the lay of the land.</i>

00:04:35.574 --> 00:04:37.543 line:0%
DR. BROOK: So this is
the park area, this forested.

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:38.778 line:0%
So this is actually

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:41.380 line:0%
the, all the movement data
from the GPS collars,

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.617 line:0%
but as you can see, there's
really significant movement.

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.886 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: One of the most
 valuable animals in the study</i>

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:50.689 line:0%
<i> is a 275-pound boar named M1.</i>

00:04:50.689 --> 00:04:52.825 line:0%
DR. BROOK: We had a GPS collar
on M01 for two years now.

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:54.326 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yep.

00:04:54.326 --> 00:04:56.829 line:0%
DR. BROOK: And M01 is outside
of the Moose Mountain

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:59.498 line:0%
and getting into farmland.

00:04:59.498 --> 00:05:03.603 line:0%
MICHELLE: M1 is so important
because he is a major traveler.

00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:06.471 line:0%
He's traveling to various
groups of females,

00:05:06.471 --> 00:05:08.407 line:0%
and he is basically
leading the researchers

00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:12.478 line:0%
right to where the other
wild boar are living.

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:14.613 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Knowing where
 the herds are hiding is key</i>

00:05:14.613 --> 00:05:17.083 line:0%
<i> in helping determine
 how many animals there are</i>

00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:19.351 line:0%
<i> and how to control them.</i>

00:05:19.351 --> 00:05:20.719 line:0%
DR. BROOK: He's our go-to guy,

00:05:20.719 --> 00:05:24.022 line:0%
but there's concerns about M01
fertilizing other females.

00:05:24.022 --> 00:05:26.592 line:0%
MICHELLE:
This M1 is an adult male,

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:29.962 line:0%
he's probably sired hundreds
of piglets if not more.

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:32.632 line:0%
So, we want to study and try
and understand these wild boar,

00:05:32.632 --> 00:05:35.034 line:0%
but we don't want him out
siring more offspring.

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:36.568 line:0%
Okay, well, it sounds like
we need to get to work.

00:05:36.568 --> 00:05:38.137 line:0%
DR. BROOK: Yes, let's do that.
Absolutely.

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:39.272 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay.

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:44.543 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: To stop M1's
 reproductive reign,</i>

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:47.480 line:0%
<i> Michelle and Shane will
 launch an aerial assault.</i>

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.081 line:0%
MICHELLE: Hello.
SHANE: Hello.

00:05:49.081 --> 00:05:52.384 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: To track down and
 sterilize the big breeder.</i>

00:05:52.384 --> 00:05:53.786 line:0%
<i> But there's a catch.</i>

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:55.020 line:0%
MICHELLE: We've got to approach
this operation

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.822 line:0%
a little differently
than we normally would.

00:05:56.822 --> 00:05:58.523 line:0%
We wouldn't want
to just castrate him,

00:05:58.523 --> 00:06:01.093 line:0%
because we want him
to have his hormones,

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:06.432 line:0%
we want him to have a drive to
find sows and other wild boar.

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:08.835 line:0%
WOMAN: Just make sure that the
tusks are hooked over the top.

00:06:08.835 --> 00:06:10.035 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay.

00:06:10.035 --> 00:06:12.705 line:0%
So, our plan is to give him
a vasectomy today.

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:14.039 line:0%
Snip, snip.

00:06:14.039 --> 00:06:15.808 line:0%
The testicles will stay in place

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:18.945 line:0%
and we'll just remove the
ability to make more piglets.

00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:25.151 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: With her gunner
 in the back seat,</i>

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:28.654 line:0%
<i> Michelle's also got
 an ace at the helm.</i>

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:30.856 line:0%
<i> Pilot Mike Reed.</i>

00:06:30.856 --> 00:06:32.090 line:0%
MICHELLE: Mike can move animals,

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:34.861 line:0%
he can put them anywhere
you want them.

00:06:38.964 --> 00:06:44.836 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But to catch M1,
 first they've got to find him.</i>

00:06:44.836 --> 00:06:46.706 line:0%
MICHELLE: Do they hold still
and just hide?

00:06:46.706 --> 00:06:47.640 line:0%
MIKE: Oh, yeah.

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:51.143 line:0%
MICHELLE: They can get into
some difficult terrain here.

00:06:51.143 --> 00:06:53.612 line:0%
They can get back in the bush.
It can be hard to find them.

00:06:53.612 --> 00:06:55.348 line:0%
They're really sneaky.

00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:59.818 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: A GPS tracking team
 is already in the sky,</i>

00:06:59.818 --> 00:07:02.655 line:0%
<i> homing in on the signal
 emitted by M1's collar</i>

00:07:02.655 --> 00:07:05.124 line:0%
<i> and reporting back
 to Michelle and the crew.</i>

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:07.660 line:0%
SHELBY: I'm starting to pick up
a faint signal here.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.796 line:0%
It's getting stronger here
on the right.

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:17.970 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: M1 is holed up
 somewhere below the chopper.</i>

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.004 line:0%
MICHELLE: We got
the coordinates,

00:07:19.004 --> 00:07:20.906 line:0%
and we're flying into the spot,

00:07:20.906 --> 00:07:23.776 line:0%
and we still can't see
the wild boar.

00:07:27.646 --> 00:07:28.814 line:0%
MICHELLE: And it's lots of snow,

00:07:28.814 --> 00:07:31.984 line:0%
a couple big open lakes,
little patches of trees.

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:34.454 line:0%
You'd think we'd
be able to see him.

00:07:35.954 --> 00:07:37.156 line:0%
Oh, here he is, right here.

00:07:40.225 --> 00:07:42.962 line:0%
Right below us.
I can see the collar, too.

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:44.964 line:0%
MIKE: Oh, yeah.

00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:46.565 line:0%
Let me know when
you're ready, Shane.

00:07:46.565 --> 00:07:47.599 line:0%
SHANE: Yeah.

00:07:47.599 --> 00:07:49.101 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: With a sight
 on the target,</i>

00:07:49.101 --> 00:07:52.738 line:0%
<i> it's up to Shane to bring him
 down with the net gun.</i>

00:07:52.738 --> 00:07:54.873 line:0%
MICHELLE: Basically now, we're
just trying to move him out

00:07:54.873 --> 00:07:56.742 line:0%
onto the ice
into a nice, open area

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:58.978 line:0%
so Shane can shoot
the net at him.

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:01.714 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But part of
 a wild boar's secret to success</i>

00:08:01.714 --> 00:08:05.084 line:0%
<i> is its ability to evade humans.</i>

00:08:05.084 --> 00:08:07.152 line:0%
MICHELLE: He knows the game.

00:08:07.152 --> 00:08:09.688 line:0%
So when he stays in the trees,
we can't get down low enough

00:08:09.688 --> 00:08:13.025 line:0%
to get a shot, so we really got
to get him out onto the ice.

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:15.661 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: To bag the boar,
 Mike must maneuver the chopper</i>

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:17.963 line:0%
<i> within 30 feet of the ground,</i>

00:08:17.963 --> 00:08:21.100 line:0%
<i> without crashing
 into the trees.</i>

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:22.535 line:0%
GARY: There he is, there.
MICHELLE: Yep.

00:08:22.535 --> 00:08:23.970 line:0%
MIKE: Has he stopped?
MICHELLE: He stopped, yep.

00:08:25.938 --> 00:08:27.206 line:0%
MICHELLE: There we go.

00:08:27.206 --> 00:08:28.273 line:0%
SHANE: Alrighty.

00:08:28.273 --> 00:08:34.913 line:0%
(music)

00:08:34.913 --> 00:08:41.787 line:0%
(music)

00:08:41.787 --> 00:08:42.788 line:0%
MICHELLE: Nice shot.

00:08:48.694 --> 00:08:51.297 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The 275-pound boar
 may be caught.</i>

00:08:51.297 --> 00:08:52.898 line:0%
SHANE: Let's go!

00:08:52.898 --> 00:08:56.736 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But he's strong,
 scared, and ready to fight.</i>

00:09:01.006 --> 00:09:02.607 line:0%
MICHELLE: Alright, go!

00:09:02.607 --> 00:09:03.976 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: To secure
 the angry pig,</i>

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:06.913 line:0%
<i> Michelle needs to hook
 the snare around M1's snout.</i>

00:09:11.816 --> 00:09:12.951 line:0%
MICHELLE: He's bleeding a bit

00:09:12.951 --> 00:09:14.720 line:0%
from where he's biting down
on the snare.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:16.354 line:0%
This is normal.
He's not hurt at all.

00:09:16.354 --> 00:09:18.190 line:0%
But we can't hold him
like this for long,

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.792 line:0%
we've really got
to get him down.

00:09:20.792 --> 00:09:22.961 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: A dose of anesthetic
 should knock him out</i>

00:09:22.961 --> 00:09:24.062 line:0%
<i> within a few minutes.</i>

00:09:24.062 --> 00:09:25.096 line:0%
MICHELLE: So, I'm just
gonna inject it.

00:09:25.096 --> 00:09:26.264 line:0%
SHANE: Watch where you put that.

00:09:26.264 --> 00:09:27.333 line:0%
MICHELLE: I can see Shane's
a little nervous

00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:28.801 line:0%
as I'm coming up
the back end there,

00:09:28.801 --> 00:09:32.905 line:0%
'cause, you know, his bum is
right by the boar's bum.

00:09:34.639 --> 00:09:36.108 line:0%
SHANE: She got it
in the right ass.

00:09:36.108 --> 00:09:38.276 line:0%
(laughs)

00:09:38.276 --> 00:09:40.612 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Now it's a waiting
 game for the drugs to hit</i>

00:09:40.612 --> 00:09:42.815 line:0%
<i> the boar's
 central nervous system.</i>

00:09:42.815 --> 00:09:44.016 line:0%
SHANE: Just hold him
to the ground.

00:09:44.016 --> 00:09:45.384 line:0%
MICHELLE:
You let him up too soon,

00:09:45.384 --> 00:09:49.088 line:0%
he'll drag us across the field,
or worse, come back at us.

00:09:49.088 --> 00:09:50.756 line:0%
This guy is really big

00:09:50.756 --> 00:09:54.126 line:0%
and so much stronger than
he looks for his size, too.

00:09:54.126 --> 00:09:56.195 line:0%
It's really important
to control their head,

00:09:56.195 --> 00:09:58.130 line:0%
because they have
four, five-inch tusks

00:09:58.130 --> 00:09:59.165 line:0%
that are sticking
out of their mouth.

00:09:59.165 --> 00:10:01.733 line:0%
They're razor-sharp.

00:10:01.733 --> 00:10:03.169 line:0%
He's still moving his tail.

00:10:06.338 --> 00:10:07.873 line:0%
Oh, (bleep).

00:10:07.873 --> 00:10:10.342 line:0%
(snorting)

00:10:10.342 --> 00:10:11.711 line:0%
No!

00:10:18.751 --> 00:10:21.653 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Wild boar are
 destroying the countryside.</i>

00:10:21.653 --> 00:10:23.188 line:0%
<i> To help curb their population,</i>

00:10:23.188 --> 00:10:26.692 line:0%
<i> Michelle needs to give this
 prolific breeder a vasectomy.</i>

00:10:29.695 --> 00:10:30.630 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, (bleep).

00:10:34.433 --> 00:10:37.236 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But his anesthetic
 isn't kicking in.</i>

00:10:41.874 --> 00:10:45.677 line:0%
SHANE: You're giving him
caffeine or something.

00:10:45.677 --> 00:10:47.212 line:0%
It's not getting to him at all.

00:10:47.212 --> 00:10:48.847 line:0%
MICHELLE: They're
super aggressive.

00:10:48.847 --> 00:10:51.117 line:0%
When they feel caught,
they will come right at you,

00:10:51.117 --> 00:10:54.853 line:0%
and, you know, there's been
some near misses already.

00:10:54.853 --> 00:10:56.421 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: They need
 to get the boar down.</i>

00:10:56.421 --> 00:10:58.223 line:0%
(bleep)

00:10:58.223 --> 00:11:01.160 line:0%
<i> But his adrenalin's
 pumping on overdrive.</i>

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:04.096 line:0%
<i> There's no choice but
 to give him more drugs.</i>

00:11:04.096 --> 00:11:06.932 line:0%
(snorting)

00:11:06.932 --> 00:11:08.200 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay.

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:14.372 line:0%
Yup.

00:11:14.372 --> 00:11:16.909 line:0%
My husband is wrestling a boar.

00:11:16.909 --> 00:11:18.911 line:0%
I'm doing some vet work.

00:11:18.911 --> 00:11:20.179 line:0%
Can't beat it.

00:11:22.247 --> 00:11:24.383 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The extra dose
 does the trick.</i>

00:11:24.383 --> 00:11:26.285 line:0%
MICHELLE: He's not fighting
anymore at all, is he?

00:11:26.285 --> 00:11:27.319 line:0%
SHANE: He's sleeping.

00:11:27.319 --> 00:11:28.821 line:0%
MICHELLE: Is he asleep?

00:11:28.821 --> 00:11:30.723 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: With M1 down and out,</i>

00:11:30.723 --> 00:11:33.058 line:0%
<i> Michelle can finally
 start his vasectomy.</i>

00:11:33.058 --> 00:11:38.297 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okey-dokey,
about to start here.

00:11:38.297 --> 00:11:40.032 line:0%
Their anatomy is just
a little bit different

00:11:40.032 --> 00:11:43.468 line:0%
from domestic pigs.

00:11:43.468 --> 00:11:44.770 line:0%
Their skin is so tough,

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:48.006 line:0%
it's like dulled my scalpel
in one cut.

00:11:48.006 --> 00:11:49.441 line:0%
Wild boar are hairy beasts,

00:11:49.441 --> 00:11:53.112 line:0%
so, you know, there isn't a ton
of hair on the scrotum itself.

00:11:53.112 --> 00:11:55.113 line:0%
A little different way
to work on them, hey?

00:11:55.113 --> 00:11:56.147 line:0%
But what's surprising to me

00:11:56.147 --> 00:12:00.085 line:0%
is how thick the hide is
on these guys.

00:12:00.085 --> 00:12:02.420 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: That leathery skin
 helps wild boar survive</i>

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:04.256 line:0%
<i> the harsh northern elements</i>

00:12:04.256 --> 00:12:07.092 line:0%
<i> and each other during
 territorial combat.</i>

00:12:07.092 --> 00:12:08.127 line:0%
MICHELLE: When they're fighting,

00:12:08.127 --> 00:12:09.494 line:0%
they really want
really thick skin

00:12:09.494 --> 00:12:13.298 line:0%
over important parts of their
body, like the testicles.

00:12:13.298 --> 00:12:15.433 line:0%
There we go, okay.

00:12:15.433 --> 00:12:18.270 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: With M1's tough
 testicles finally open,</i>

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:21.907 line:0%
<i> Michelle feels inside.</i>

00:12:21.907 --> 00:12:26.077 line:0%
MICHELLE: Now it's kind of
a fishing expedition to find it.

00:12:26.077 --> 00:12:29.314 line:0%
I'm getting a little bit
of the vas deferens,

00:12:29.314 --> 00:12:34.220 line:0%
which takes the semen
from the testicle out.

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:35.887 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Michelle removes
 a half centimeter</i>

00:12:35.887 --> 00:12:39.791 line:0%
<i> of the vas deferens,
 then ties it off.</i>

00:12:39.791 --> 00:12:43.161 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay.
One down and one to go.

00:12:43.161 --> 00:12:45.798 line:0%
This animal will still have
all his hormones.

00:12:45.798 --> 00:12:47.265 line:0%
He's gonna go back
out in the wild.

00:12:47.265 --> 00:12:49.068 line:0%
He's gonna find lots
of other groups of pigs,

00:12:49.068 --> 00:12:51.470 line:0%
and he's gonna save this team
hundreds of hours

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:54.306 line:0%
in terms of looking
for wild boar.

00:12:54.306 --> 00:12:56.308 line:0%
He's still all boy.

00:12:56.308 --> 00:12:57.810 line:0%
And these are
absorbable sutures,

00:12:57.810 --> 00:12:59.845 line:0%
so he won't have to come back
and see me next week

00:12:59.845 --> 00:13:01.513 line:0%
to get them out.

00:13:01.513 --> 00:13:04.549 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: One final injection
 will reverse the anesthesia</i>

00:13:04.549 --> 00:13:06.551 line:0%
<i> and wake the sleeping giant.</i>

00:13:06.551 --> 00:13:10.122 line:0%
MICHELLE: Usually it takes,
you know, four to ten minutes

00:13:10.122 --> 00:13:12.390 line:0%
for them to get up
after the reversal,

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:15.060 line:0%
but sometimes it can be
a little bit faster.

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:16.228 line:0%
Okay.

00:13:19.031 --> 00:13:20.299 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 With darkness falling,</i>

00:13:20.299 --> 00:13:23.068 line:0%
<i> there's no time to wait
 for the boar to come to.</i>

00:13:27.206 --> 00:13:30.009 line:0%
MICHELLE: Usually we wait until
the animal's head up at least,

00:13:30.009 --> 00:13:32.210 line:0%
but we're out of daylight
and for safety's sake,

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:34.346 line:0%
we've got to get out of here.

00:13:34.346 --> 00:13:36.581 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But the GPS team
 is still in the sky</i>

00:13:36.581 --> 00:13:38.950 line:0%
<i> keeping tabs on M1.</i>

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:41.486 line:0%
<i> When they hear the telltale
 signal from of his collar,</i>

00:13:41.486 --> 00:13:43.989 line:0%
<i> they'll know he's back
 up and at 'em.</i>

00:13:47.225 --> 00:13:48.260 line:0%
MICHELLE: Got him!

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:49.195 line:0%
MIKE: Got him!

00:13:54.499 --> 00:13:57.870 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Somewhere in the bush
 below, M1 is on the move again.</i>

00:13:57.870 --> 00:13:59.003 line:0%
MICHELLE: He's okay.

00:13:59.003 --> 00:14:02.274 line:0%
He's got himself back up
to body temperature,

00:14:02.274 --> 00:14:04.643 line:0%
and there will be
no more babies.

00:14:04.643 --> 00:14:10.415 line:0%
(music)

00:14:10.415 --> 00:14:12.450 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Michelle will be
 back to Saskatchewan</i>

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:16.088 line:0%
<i> to knock other super breeders
 out of commission.</i>

00:14:16.088 --> 00:14:17.455 line:0%
MICHELLE: Willow and Shane
are going to take

00:14:17.455 --> 00:14:19.190 line:0%
a little daddy-daughter time,

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:23.094 line:0%
and we will all meet up
back at home.

00:14:23.094 --> 00:14:24.596 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But for
 this traveling vet,</i>

00:14:24.596 --> 00:14:26.131 line:0%
<i> home will have to wait.</i>

00:14:26.131 --> 00:14:27.933 line:0%
<i> She's on her way
 to help a friend</i>

00:14:27.933 --> 00:14:30.169 line:0%
<i> who's hauling
 some unusual cargo.</i>

00:14:30.169 --> 00:14:32.103 line:0%
MICHELLE: Mike Miller and
his wife, Kelly, called

00:14:32.103 --> 00:14:35.507 line:0%
because they're moving some
caribou from British Columbia

00:14:35.507 --> 00:14:37.109 line:0%
to the Alaska Wildlife
Conservation Center

00:14:37.109 --> 00:14:38.543 line:0%
where they're from.

00:14:38.543 --> 00:14:39.912 line:0%
They've asked me to come along,

00:14:39.912 --> 00:14:42.080 line:0%
and I think it's actually
a really good idea

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:43.382 line:0%
to have a veterinarian along.

00:14:43.382 --> 00:14:44.983 line:0%
Hey, guys!

00:14:44.983 --> 00:14:46.151 line:0%
MIKE: Michelle!
MICHELLE: Hey!

00:14:46.151 --> 00:14:47.552 line:0%
KELLY: How's it going?
MIKE: Good to see you!

00:14:47.552 --> 00:14:50.989 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah, nice to see you!
MIKE: Yeah.

00:14:50.989 --> 00:14:53.258 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Standing between
 the caribou and their new home</i>

00:14:53.258 --> 00:14:56.094 line:0%
<i> are nearly 1,500 miles
 of open road.</i>

00:14:56.094 --> 00:14:57.262 line:0%
KELLY: Yeah, the older ones
are in the front.

00:14:57.262 --> 00:14:58.430 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay.
MIKE: The bigger ones.

00:14:58.430 --> 00:14:59.965 line:0%
KELLY: And then all the calves
are in the back.

00:14:59.965 --> 00:15:01.100 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay.

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:02.501 line:0%
MIKE: These caribou
are being donated

00:15:02.501 --> 00:15:05.204 line:0%
by the British Columbia
government.

00:15:05.204 --> 00:15:06.672 line:0%
They did dietary studies.

00:15:06.672 --> 00:15:09.207 line:0%
Taking them out, letting them
loose, and see what they eat.

00:15:09.207 --> 00:15:10.976 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah,
these guys look good.

00:15:10.976 --> 00:15:14.212 line:0%
MIKE: They had some extra ones
that they wanted us to adopt,

00:15:14.212 --> 00:15:16.948 line:0%
and that's what started
this whole process.

00:15:16.948 --> 00:15:18.216 line:0%
MICHELLE: How's it going so far?

00:15:18.216 --> 00:15:19.618 line:0%
MIKE: We're half a day into it.

00:15:19.618 --> 00:15:22.655 line:0%
We got, like, a day left and
we're just getting nervous.

00:15:22.655 --> 00:15:25.190 line:0%
MICHELLE: It's big deal to take
caribou anywhere,

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:26.457 line:0%
but for that long of a trip,

00:15:26.457 --> 00:15:29.028 line:0%
if there are any issues
that happen at all,

00:15:29.028 --> 00:15:31.163 line:0%
you know, it's very difficult
to deal with them.

00:15:31.163 --> 00:15:32.197 line:0%
KELLY: It's a long trip,
you know.

00:15:32.197 --> 00:15:33.298 line:0%
MICHELLE: It is a long trip.

00:15:33.298 --> 00:15:35.100 line:0%
KELLY: It's longer than
they've ever gone.

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:36.301 line:0%
MIKE: The drive is
pretty intimidating

00:15:36.301 --> 00:15:37.702 line:0%
when you think about it.

00:15:37.702 --> 00:15:40.973 line:0%
You know, all the way across
the country with wild animals.

00:15:40.973 --> 00:15:42.474 line:0%
Boy, if we ever need any,
have any trouble,

00:15:42.474 --> 00:15:45.443 line:0%
we wouldn't know where to begin,
and that's why we need Michelle.

00:15:45.443 --> 00:15:46.678 line:0%
MICHELLE: You got enough snacks?

00:15:46.678 --> 00:15:48.647 line:0%
I'm only going
if there's good snacks.

00:15:54.652 --> 00:15:56.755 line:0%
Okay, everybody is looking good.

00:15:56.755 --> 00:15:58.757 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The trailer is
 equipped with four cameras,</i>

00:15:58.757 --> 00:16:02.494 line:0%
<i> so the team can keep an eye
 on their precious cargo.</i>

00:16:02.494 --> 00:16:04.362 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay,
you get some sleep.

00:16:04.362 --> 00:16:06.364 line:0%
KELLY: I'm gonna try.
MICHELLE: Alright.

00:16:06.364 --> 00:16:08.233 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But in case
 of emergency,</i>

00:16:08.233 --> 00:16:10.402 line:0%
<i> they'll have limited options.</i>

00:16:10.402 --> 00:16:12.770 line:0%
<i> International regulations
 require that the animals</i>

00:16:12.770 --> 00:16:16.508 line:0%
<i> remain inside the trailer until
 they get to the Alaskan border.</i>

00:16:16.508 --> 00:16:17.676 line:0%
MIKE: There's a special seal

00:16:17.676 --> 00:16:20.044 line:0%
that the Canadian Food
Inspection Agency puts

00:16:20.044 --> 00:16:21.713 line:0%
on all the doors of the trailer,

00:16:21.713 --> 00:16:25.083 line:0%
and they just want to ensure
that you don't let the animals

00:16:25.083 --> 00:16:28.453 line:0%
accidentally get loose until
the time you get to the border.

00:16:28.453 --> 00:16:32.157 line:0%
So there's no getting into the
trailer if something goes wrong.

00:16:32.157 --> 00:16:33.258 line:0%
Even if one was bleeding,

00:16:33.258 --> 00:16:37.029 line:0%
I don't think you
could break that seal.

00:16:37.029 --> 00:16:38.730 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: If the seal
 shows signs of tampering</i>

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:43.435 line:0%
<i> at the border crossing,
 the caribou will be sent back.</i>

00:16:45.537 --> 00:16:49.107 line:0%
MIKE: I hope they're enjoying
the trip as much as we are.

00:16:49.107 --> 00:16:50.209 line:0%
They need to just relax.

00:16:50.209 --> 00:16:51.309 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah.

00:16:51.309 --> 00:16:53.645 line:0%
MIKE: It'll be over
before they know it.

00:16:53.645 --> 00:16:55.046 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 Before the trip began,</i>

00:16:55.046 --> 00:16:56.815 line:0%
<i> the caribou were given
 a tranquilizer</i>

00:16:56.815 --> 00:16:59.517 line:0%
<i> to help them endure the ride.</i>

00:16:59.517 --> 00:17:01.020 line:0%
MICHELLE: This particular
tranquilizer,

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:03.454 line:0%
it really just takes
the stress and anxiety away

00:17:03.454 --> 00:17:05.156 line:0%
from, you know, their psyche,

00:17:05.156 --> 00:17:07.693 line:0%
so they can just kind of
relax and chill out,

00:17:07.693 --> 00:17:09.962 line:0%
and get through the trip.

00:17:11.830 --> 00:17:17.703 line:0%
(music)

00:17:17.703 --> 00:17:18.737 line:0%
I'm wired.

00:17:18.737 --> 00:17:20.839 line:0%
If you need me to drive,
I'm ready.

00:17:20.839 --> 00:17:23.208 line:0%
MIKE: I'm putting in my time now
when it's daylight.

00:17:23.208 --> 00:17:25.076 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah.

00:17:25.076 --> 00:17:28.146 line:0%
So you might think a long trip
like this would be boring,

00:17:28.146 --> 00:17:31.249 line:0%
but we're kind of mixing up
who's driving,

00:17:31.249 --> 00:17:34.119 line:0%
and there's no shortage
of things to see.

00:17:34.119 --> 00:17:35.153 line:0%
MIKE: Caribou!

00:17:35.153 --> 00:17:36.187 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, yeah!
There's five.

00:17:36.187 --> 00:17:37.423 line:0%
MIKE: Hey, they're looking at us

00:17:37.423 --> 00:17:40.158 line:0%
as if they know we have
their cousins in the car.

00:17:40.158 --> 00:17:41.793 line:0%
MICHELLE: Having some snacks,
lots of snacks.

00:17:41.793 --> 00:17:44.663 line:0%
It's just such an amazing trip.

00:17:44.663 --> 00:17:46.498 line:0%
A moose!

00:17:46.498 --> 00:17:52.537 line:0%
(imitates moose call)

00:17:52.537 --> 00:17:54.873 line:0%
We're not too far into the trip
and I'm watching the monitor,

00:17:54.873 --> 00:17:56.708 line:0%
and as I'm looking at it,

00:17:56.708 --> 00:17:59.478 line:0%
I see something that,
that is not right.

00:18:02.413 --> 00:18:04.550 line:0%
This one is looking
a little funny, Mike.

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:05.750 line:0%
MIKE: Oh, yeah.

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:07.251 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, (bleep), yeah,
she is backing up.

00:18:07.251 --> 00:18:10.622 line:0%
So, she just keeps backing up
and digging.

00:18:10.622 --> 00:18:12.391 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Backing up and
 digging may be signs</i>

00:18:12.391 --> 00:18:16.494 line:0%
<i> that the long-term tranquilizer
 isn't sitting right.</i>

00:18:16.494 --> 00:18:17.529 line:0%
MICHELLE: There can be
side effects

00:18:17.529 --> 00:18:19.364 line:0%
with these tranquilizers.

00:18:19.364 --> 00:18:21.266 line:0%
They protect most
of the animals,

00:18:21.266 --> 00:18:22.634 line:0%
but they're all individuals,

00:18:22.634 --> 00:18:26.237 line:0%
and there are some then that
will react in a strange way.

00:18:26.237 --> 00:18:28.307 line:0%
You see how she's just
constantly digging?

00:18:28.307 --> 00:18:29.674 line:0%
KELLY: Oh, yeah.

00:18:29.674 --> 00:18:31.543 line:0%
MICHELLE: And her head is down
and she's kind of like...

00:18:31.543 --> 00:18:34.145 line:0%
MIKE: Oh, my gosh.

00:18:34.145 --> 00:18:35.747 line:0%
MICHELLE:
She's frantically pawing,

00:18:35.747 --> 00:18:37.582 line:0%
and then she's dragging
her face,

00:18:37.582 --> 00:18:39.350 line:0%
and not just a little bit
of a scrape,

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:41.153 line:0%
I mean, she's like
dragging her face

00:18:41.153 --> 00:18:42.520 line:0%
as hard as she can
on the ground.

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.257 line:0%
And if she continues to do that,
she could cause abrasions

00:18:45.257 --> 00:18:48.260 line:0%
and injuries to her teeth
or her skin.

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:49.628 line:0%
MIKE: Well, it looks like
she's...

00:18:49.628 --> 00:18:51.296 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah, like, she's
progressing then, you know?

00:18:51.296 --> 00:18:52.597 line:0%
MIKE: Yeah. You can...

00:18:52.597 --> 00:18:55.400 line:0%
MICHELLE: No, we need to find
somewhere to pull over.

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:57.169 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Without immediate
 intervention,</i>

00:18:57.169 --> 00:18:58.737 line:0%
<i> the caribou's
 repetitive behavior</i>

00:18:58.737 --> 00:19:01.840 line:0%
<i> could cause her heart rate
 and body temperature to spike</i>

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:03.708 line:0%
<i> and lead to sudden death.</i>

00:19:03.708 --> 00:19:05.510 line:0%
MICHELLE: I definitely
need to intervene.

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:08.346 line:0%
But because we have a sealed
trailer for crossing the border,

00:19:08.346 --> 00:19:10.482 line:0%
I can't get in there and get
my hands on this animal

00:19:10.482 --> 00:19:11.783 line:0%
like I would normally.

00:19:11.783 --> 00:19:13.752 line:0%
MIKE: Yeah, that's not good.

00:19:13.752 --> 00:19:15.553 line:0%
MICHELLE: It's got to stop,
she's gonna hurt herself.

00:19:15.553 --> 00:19:17.923 line:0%
I need to do something.

00:19:17.923 --> 00:19:19.625 line:0%
That's awful!

00:19:26.698 --> 00:19:29.701 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: 11 caribou are
 on a 37-hour journey</i>

00:19:29.701 --> 00:19:32.637 line:0%
<i> to an animal sanctuary
 in Southern Alaska.</i>

00:19:32.637 --> 00:19:35.940 line:0%
<i> Each was given a tranquilizer
 to help it endure the trip.</i>

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:39.978 line:0%
<i> But one of them is having a
 dangerous reaction to the drug.</i>

00:19:39.978 --> 00:19:41.446 line:0%
MIKE: Yeah, that's not good.

00:19:41.446 --> 00:19:43.615 line:0%
MICHELLE: She's pawing
in kind of a manic way,

00:19:43.615 --> 00:19:45.917 line:0%
and now she's starting
to drag her nose.

00:19:45.917 --> 00:19:47.819 line:0%
She's really tensing up,

00:19:47.819 --> 00:19:50.589 line:0%
and that will actually start
to cause her to overheat.

00:19:50.589 --> 00:19:52.491 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Left untreated,
 the caribou's heart</i>

00:19:52.491 --> 00:19:55.727 line:0%
<i> and respiratory rates could
 climb out of control,</i>

00:19:55.727 --> 00:19:57.896 line:0%
<i> leading to sudden death.</i>

00:19:57.896 --> 00:20:01.299 line:0%
<i> To stop her spiral, the caribou
 must receive an injection</i>

00:20:01.299 --> 00:20:04.836 line:0%
<i> of midazolam,
 a Valium-like sedative.</i>

00:20:04.836 --> 00:20:06.971 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay, we're coming.

00:20:06.971 --> 00:20:10.241 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But there's no easy
 way to administer the drug.</i>

00:20:10.241 --> 00:20:11.342 line:0%
MIKE: Canada seals the trailer

00:20:11.342 --> 00:20:13.945 line:0%
and you're not allowed
to break the seal.

00:20:13.945 --> 00:20:15.247 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The team
 is prohibited</i>

00:20:15.247 --> 00:20:17.014 line:0%
<i> from opening the trailer doors.</i>

00:20:17.014 --> 00:20:18.250 line:0%
MICHELLE: We'll just have to try

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:20.252 line:0%
and get it through
the hole here.

00:20:30.728 --> 00:20:33.498 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, yeah,
you're looking rough.

00:20:33.498 --> 00:20:35.700 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: With only a couple
 of inches to maneuver,</i>

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:37.269 line:0%
<i> Michelle must inject the needle</i>

00:20:37.269 --> 00:20:39.705 line:0%
<i> squarely into
 the caribou's flank.</i>

00:20:41.473 --> 00:20:44.576 line:0%
MICHELLE: So I'm doing the best
I can with this tiny little slot

00:20:44.576 --> 00:20:45.844 line:0%
in the side of the trailer,

00:20:45.844 --> 00:20:47.278 line:0%
but it's really tricky

00:20:47.278 --> 00:20:49.581 line:0%
to try and maneuver
the stick pole in there,

00:20:49.581 --> 00:20:52.317 line:0%
and, you know, actually inject
in the right spot in the animal

00:20:52.317 --> 00:20:53.785 line:0%
and not injure any
of the other caribou

00:20:53.785 --> 00:20:56.221 line:0%
that are milling around
in the area.

00:20:58.323 --> 00:20:59.992 line:0%
Move your head.

00:20:59.992 --> 00:21:01.426 line:7%
Move your head.

00:21:07.498 --> 00:21:10.769 line:0%
It did not, it did not inject.
Dang it.

00:21:10.769 --> 00:21:12.070 line:0%
MIKE: Do you want
another syringe?

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:13.371 line:0%
MICHELLE: No, it's all
still in there,

00:21:13.371 --> 00:21:15.039 line:0%
but I want another needle.

00:21:15.039 --> 00:21:18.510 line:0%
The first needle just bent a bit
because she started to run.

00:21:20.578 --> 00:21:22.013 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Every second
 the caribou goes</i>

00:21:22.013 --> 00:21:26.851 line:0%
<i> without the injection, her
 self-inflicted blows get worse.</i>

00:21:26.851 --> 00:21:30.255 line:0%
MICHELLE: Like, she's dragging
her face really hard.

00:21:34.025 --> 00:21:35.594 line:0%
MIKE: How'd that one go?

00:21:35.594 --> 00:21:38.029 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah, got that one.
MIKE: Alright.

00:21:38.029 --> 00:21:41.099 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The full dose
 is finally in.</i>

00:21:41.099 --> 00:21:42.968 line:0%
MICHELLE: She should
start to slow down.

00:21:42.968 --> 00:21:45.037 line:0%
She's still going,
you can see her.

00:21:45.037 --> 00:21:48.073 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But it doesn't
 seem to be working.</i>

00:21:48.073 --> 00:21:50.075 line:0%
KELLY: Boy, he doesn't look
that much better so far.

00:21:50.075 --> 00:21:51.710 line:0%
MICHELLE: No. So why don't
we drive for a bit

00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:53.245 line:0%
to try and cool her down?

00:21:56.748 --> 00:21:58.449 line:0%
I'm hoping in
the next few minutes

00:21:58.449 --> 00:22:00.618 line:0%
we see a change in her behavior.

00:22:00.618 --> 00:22:01.853 line:0%
If this doesn't work,

00:22:01.853 --> 00:22:05.823 line:0%
I'm gonna have to get in there
and get my hands on her.

00:22:05.823 --> 00:22:07.925 line:0%
Okay, it's been 14 minutes.

00:22:07.925 --> 00:22:09.827 line:0%
If I have to break the seal
and go in there,

00:22:09.827 --> 00:22:11.129 line:0%
that's it, shipment's over.

00:22:11.129 --> 00:22:12.497 line:0%
They can't go into
the United States.

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:19.003 line:0%
The pawing has stopped.

00:22:19.003 --> 00:22:23.074 line:0%
Oh, she looks way better,
okay, oh, thank God. Uhh.

00:22:23.074 --> 00:22:24.408 line:0%
Uhh.

00:22:24.408 --> 00:22:25.476 line:0%
I thought this was gonna be
a boring drive

00:22:25.476 --> 00:22:26.811 line:0%
where I slept the whole way.

00:22:26.811 --> 00:22:28.046 line:0%
MIKE: Yeah.

00:22:28.046 --> 00:22:29.948 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The drug should have
 a calming effect</i>

00:22:29.948 --> 00:22:31.816 line:0%
<i> for four to six hours,</i>

00:22:31.816 --> 00:22:34.920 line:0%
<i> but for Michelle, there's
 no relaxing on the job.</i>

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:36.854 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah, I don't want
to take my eyes off that.

00:22:36.854 --> 00:22:38.556 line:0%
Yeah.

00:22:38.556 --> 00:22:41.860 line:0%
You know, kind of the air
of road trip, snacks, and fun

00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:44.329 line:0%
is gone until
we cross the border.

00:22:47.732 --> 00:22:50.835 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: By the evening,
 all passengers are hanging on,</i>

00:22:50.835 --> 00:22:52.904 line:0%
<i> but it's touch and go.</i>

00:22:52.904 --> 00:22:54.706 line:0%
KELLY: That one is jumping.

00:22:56.107 --> 00:22:57.876 line:0%
(bleep)

00:22:57.876 --> 00:22:59.411 line:0%
MICHELLE: It's a powder keg
in there.

00:22:59.411 --> 00:23:01.679 line:0%
I mean, these caribou are
not used to being confined.

00:23:01.679 --> 00:23:03.982 line:0%
And they're all kind of starting
to look a little crazy,

00:23:03.982 --> 00:23:06.717 line:0%
they want to get out.

00:23:06.717 --> 00:23:08.052 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Michelle uses
 a pit stop</i>

00:23:08.052 --> 00:23:10.688 line:0%
<i> to try to soothe the herd.</i>

00:23:10.688 --> 00:23:13.191 line:0%
MICHELLE: Caribou candy.

00:23:13.191 --> 00:23:15.626 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Since they're
 not yet in Alaska,</i>

00:23:15.626 --> 00:23:17.595 line:0%
<i> international regulations
 prohibit her</i>

00:23:17.595 --> 00:23:19.965 line:0%
<i> from opening the trailer doors.</i>

00:23:19.965 --> 00:23:22.533 line:0%
<i> So she delivers dinner
 drive-in style.</i>

00:23:22.533 --> 00:23:23.835 line:0%
MICHELLE: But I pulled all this,

00:23:23.835 --> 00:23:27.505 line:0%
and they're chewing it
immediately, so something to do.

00:23:27.505 --> 00:23:32.744 line:0%
They got the munchies, the road
trip munchies just like we do.

00:23:32.744 --> 00:23:34.746 line:0%
There you go.

00:23:34.746 --> 00:23:35.680 line:0%
Bon appetit.

00:23:42.620 --> 00:23:45.690 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The willows satiate
 the caribou through the night.</i>

00:23:48.092 --> 00:23:52.964 line:0%
<i> And all morning long until they
 reach their new home state.</i>

00:23:52.964 --> 00:23:55.367 line:0%
(clapping)

00:24:01.673 --> 00:24:02.840 line:0%
KELLY: I know, right?

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:04.475 line:0%
MICHELLE: We're all exhausted,

00:24:04.475 --> 00:24:09.047 line:0%
but the good news is
we're in the homestretch.

00:24:09.047 --> 00:24:12.884 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: After 37 hours
 and 1,600 miles,</i>

00:24:12.884 --> 00:24:14.586 line:0%
<i> the caribou finally arrive</i>

00:24:14.586 --> 00:24:17.522 line:0%
<i> at the Alaska Wildlife
 Conservation Center,</i>

00:24:17.522 --> 00:24:20.525 line:0%
<i> where they'll roam free
 in a 15-acre enclosure.</i>

00:24:20.525 --> 00:24:21.759 line:0%
MIKE: Here's the turnoff,
I can see it.

00:24:21.759 --> 00:24:22.827 line:0%
KELLY: Yay!

00:24:22.827 --> 00:24:25.664 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, awesome. Yay.

00:24:30.501 --> 00:24:32.670 line:0%
I mean, they've been
in this little tin can

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:37.542 line:0%
for 30 plus hours, so I want
to watch them as they unload

00:24:37.542 --> 00:24:39.744 line:0%
to make sure I don't see
any signs of limping,

00:24:39.744 --> 00:24:41.846 line:0%
lacerations, or injuries.

00:24:41.846 --> 00:24:45.149 line:0%
Okay, we're almost done,
as long as everybody looks good.

00:24:45.149 --> 00:24:46.851 line:0%
You just never know
what's gonna happen

00:24:46.851 --> 00:24:49.587 line:0%
when you release an animal until
they step out of the trailer

00:24:49.587 --> 00:24:51.622 line:0%
and start exploring
their new home.

00:24:51.622 --> 00:24:53.224 line:0%
MIKE: Everybody good?

00:24:53.224 --> 00:24:55.227 line:0%
Here we go.

00:24:55.227 --> 00:24:56.628 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh.

00:24:56.628 --> 00:24:57.662 line:0%
Come on, everybody.

00:24:57.662 --> 00:24:58.730 line:0%
MIKE: Come on.

00:25:04.602 --> 00:25:06.671 line:0%
MIKE: Here we go.

00:25:06.671 --> 00:25:09.641 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: After traveling
 37 hours cross-country,</i>

00:25:09.641 --> 00:25:12.076 line:0%
<i> 11 caribou have made it
 to their new home</i>

00:25:12.076 --> 00:25:14.678 line:0%
<i> at the Alaska Wildlife
 Conservation Center.</i>

00:25:14.678 --> 00:25:15.914 line:0%
MICHELLE: Come on, everybody.

00:25:15.914 --> 00:25:17.582 line:0%
MIKE: Come on.

00:25:17.582 --> 00:25:20.885 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Now, it's time for
 their first taste of freedom.</i>

00:25:20.885 --> 00:25:21.820 line:0%
MICHELLE: Come on, guys.

00:25:25.990 --> 00:25:27.158 line:0%
MIKE: It's like they fell asleep

00:25:27.158 --> 00:25:29.060 line:0%
and woke up in a foreign country
or something.

00:25:29.060 --> 00:25:30.561 line:0%
They don't know
how they got there,

00:25:30.561 --> 00:25:33.498 line:0%
so they're a little leery
of their new surroundings.

00:25:34.632 --> 00:25:39.104 line:0%
(banging on trailer)

00:25:39.104 --> 00:25:42.039 line:0%
MICHELLE: Nice.
They look amazing.

00:25:42.039 --> 00:25:43.675 line:0%
MIKE: Once we let them loose,

00:25:43.675 --> 00:25:46.077 line:0%
I really felt like we really
accomplished something,

00:25:46.077 --> 00:25:48.179 line:0%
and it just felt good
to see them come out,

00:25:48.179 --> 00:25:50.581 line:0%
and to run around,
and to be free.

00:25:50.581 --> 00:25:53.618 line:0%
MICHELLE: They look super
bright, there's nobody limping,

00:25:53.618 --> 00:25:54.986 line:0%
they're all excited,
looking around,

00:25:54.986 --> 00:25:56.788 line:0%
they're even nibbling
the grass already.

00:25:56.788 --> 00:25:58.723 line:0%
Oh, here come the mamas.

00:25:58.723 --> 00:26:01.559 line:0%
MIKE: Here they come.

00:26:01.559 --> 00:26:03.361 line:0%
MICHELLE: Hi, guys!

00:26:03.361 --> 00:26:05.730 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The female that
 had everyone so worried</i>

00:26:05.730 --> 00:26:08.165 line:0%
<i> is back to her old self.</i>

00:26:08.165 --> 00:26:09.867 line:0%
MICHELLE: That was
a long trip, hey?

00:26:09.867 --> 00:26:10.902 line:0%
KELLY: Yay!

00:26:10.902 --> 00:26:13.071 line:0%
MIKE: Their new home!

00:26:13.071 --> 00:26:15.739 line:0%
Hey, hey!

00:26:15.739 --> 00:26:17.074 line:0%
KELLY: Where's the champagne?

00:26:17.074 --> 00:26:19.844 line:0%
MICHELLE: Nice driving.
Nice driving.

00:26:19.844 --> 00:26:21.746 line:0%
I'm really proud to be
a part of this,

00:26:21.746 --> 00:26:25.650 line:0%
of bringing these caribou,
getting them a new home.

00:26:25.650 --> 00:26:28.018 line:0%
It's been a journey
in all senses,

00:26:28.018 --> 00:26:30.121 line:0%
and I'm glad it's gonna
have a happy ending.

00:26:30.121 --> 00:26:33.692 line:0%
Hot shower! So excited!

00:26:40.898 --> 00:26:42.233 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: With the new day,</i>

00:26:42.233 --> 00:26:45.036 line:0%
<i> Michelle doesn't have
 the option of sleeping in.</i>

00:26:45.036 --> 00:26:47.772 line:0%
<i> She's up with the sun
 in Haines, Alaska,</i>

00:26:47.772 --> 00:26:50.875 line:0%
<i> where duty calls at the
 American Bald Eagle Foundation.</i>

00:26:50.875 --> 00:26:52.343 line:0%
(dogs barking)

00:26:52.343 --> 00:26:54.078 line:0%
MICHELLE: Alright, buddy, buddy!

00:26:54.078 --> 00:26:57.215 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: At her clinic, only
 a wall separates her clients</i>

00:26:57.215 --> 00:27:00.017 line:0%
<i> and some of the deadliest
 predators on Earth.</i>

00:27:00.017 --> 00:27:01.819 line:0%
MICHELLE: I just had to lock
the door into the eagle pen,

00:27:01.819 --> 00:27:03.321 line:0%
because everyone likes
to leave the office

00:27:03.321 --> 00:27:06.858 line:0%
and walk into the eagle pen
with their little Chihuahua.

00:27:06.858 --> 00:27:08.859 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Here, Michelle
 performs regular checkups</i>

00:27:08.859 --> 00:27:10.661 line:0%
<i> on all the birds of prey.</i>

00:27:10.661 --> 00:27:11.963 line:0%
MICHELLE: Hey! How's Bentley?

00:27:11.963 --> 00:27:14.899 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But today's
 for the dogs.</i>

00:27:14.899 --> 00:27:16.400 line:0%
WOMAN: He jumped off
of our porch.

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:18.303 line:0%
It's about a 10-foot drop
probably.

00:27:18.303 --> 00:27:19.671 line:0%
MICHELLE: Whoa.
WOMAN: And he just kind of...

00:27:19.671 --> 00:27:21.138 line:0%
MICHELLE: Hey! How's it going?
WOMAN: Hello.

00:27:21.138 --> 00:27:23.775 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Clinic doors are open
 for any pooch with a problem.</i>

00:27:23.775 --> 00:27:26.344 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, this is
a definite puppy pile.

00:27:26.344 --> 00:27:27.879 line:0%
Our pets are just like
little kids;

00:27:27.879 --> 00:27:30.114 line:0%
going to the doctor is
not their favorite thing.

00:27:30.114 --> 00:27:32.083 line:0%
(yelps)

00:27:32.083 --> 00:27:34.685 line:0%
If he whips his head up,
it could break your nose.

00:27:34.685 --> 00:27:37.455 line:0%
If they pick up even a sniff
of the vet stink,

00:27:37.455 --> 00:27:40.758 line:0%
they often start quivering
right away.

00:27:40.758 --> 00:27:41.826 line:0%
Lay down, sweets.

00:27:41.826 --> 00:27:42.927 line:0%
(dog farts)

00:27:42.927 --> 00:27:44.162 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, sad.
TRACY: She's so scared.

00:27:44.162 --> 00:27:45.430 line:0%
MICHELLE: That wasn't me,
I swear.

00:27:45.430 --> 00:27:47.999 line:0%
See, just the top bit
of the nail there is split.

00:27:47.999 --> 00:27:49.033 line:0%
(yelps)

00:27:49.033 --> 00:27:50.435 line:0%
Oh, Bentley, sorry, sorry.

00:27:50.435 --> 00:27:52.269 line:0%
The most dramatic
nail trim ever.

00:27:52.269 --> 00:27:54.405 line:0%
I just do my best
to help them relax

00:27:54.405 --> 00:27:56.207 line:0%
and make it
a positive experience.

00:27:56.207 --> 00:27:57.708 line:0%
Oh, that looks tender.

00:27:57.708 --> 00:27:58.943 line:0%
We're gonna give her
some antibiotics

00:27:58.943 --> 00:28:00.344 line:0%
to help her along with this.

00:28:00.344 --> 00:28:03.448 line:0%
But sometimes I just have
to deal with the fallout.

00:28:03.448 --> 00:28:05.950 line:0%
(dog farting)

00:28:05.950 --> 00:28:08.219 line:0%
Oof. Oof.

00:28:08.219 --> 00:28:10.155 line:0%
Poor Sasha.

00:28:11.822 --> 00:28:15.259 line:0%
DAVE: Come on.
Come on, Homer, listen up.

00:28:15.259 --> 00:28:16.393 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Michelle's
 next patient</i>

00:28:16.393 --> 00:28:18.429 line:0%
<i> is a five-month-old coonhound,</i>

00:28:18.429 --> 00:28:20.998 line:0%
<i> with a mind and nose
 of his own.</i>

00:28:20.998 --> 00:28:22.901 line:0%
DAVE: Come on, buddy!

00:28:22.901 --> 00:28:24.168 line:0%
Come on, buddy!

00:28:24.168 --> 00:28:26.337 line:0%
Homer, get over. Hey!

00:28:26.337 --> 00:28:30.208 line:0%
He didn't get no breakfast
today, so he's like scouring.

00:28:30.208 --> 00:28:32.110 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Coonhounds are
 known for possessing</i>

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:35.113 line:0%
<i> one of the most sensitive
 sniffers in the canine kingdom.</i>

00:28:35.113 --> 00:28:38.383 line:0%
DAVE: Let's go. Come on.
Let's go see the doctor.

00:28:38.383 --> 00:28:39.951 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Dave Stickler
 has kept the breed</i>

00:28:39.951 --> 00:28:41.886 line:0%
<i> for over 15 years,</i>

00:28:41.886 --> 00:28:44.089 line:0%
<i> but almost as soon as
 he brought Homer home,</i>

00:28:44.089 --> 00:28:46.357 line:0%
<i> he became concerned that
 something was wrong.</i>

00:28:46.357 --> 00:28:47.524 line:0%
DAVE: Come on.

00:28:47.524 --> 00:28:49.494 line:0%
I noticed he had a little bump
on his neck,

00:28:49.494 --> 00:28:51.495 line:0%
and then all of a sudden,
it's like growing,

00:28:51.495 --> 00:28:52.963 line:0%
and I'm going like, huh,

00:28:52.963 --> 00:28:55.834 line:0%
I'm bringing this guy in
to see the doc.

00:28:55.834 --> 00:28:57.001 line:0%
Come on, Huck.

00:28:57.001 --> 00:28:58.102 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: His friend
 Huckleberry</i>

00:28:58.102 --> 00:29:01.306 line:0%
<i> recently died of old age.</i>

00:29:01.306 --> 00:29:04.275 line:0%
<i> And his 17-month-old pup,
 also named Homer,</i>

00:29:04.275 --> 00:29:06.177 line:0%
<i> succumbed to cancer.</i>

00:29:06.177 --> 00:29:07.278 line:0%
DAVE: Call me a worrywart,

00:29:07.278 --> 00:29:11.182 line:0%
but I'm kind of concerned
about my puppy.

00:29:12.116 --> 00:29:13.384 line:0%
Come on, Homer. Come on.

00:29:13.384 --> 00:29:15.920 line:0%
MICHELLE: Hi! How's it going?

00:29:15.920 --> 00:29:18.022 line:0%
DAVE: Hi! Homer's got him
something going on.

00:29:18.022 --> 00:29:19.057 line:0%
MICHELLE: Uh-oh.

00:29:19.057 --> 00:29:20.791 line:0%
DAVE: I had a flea collar
on him.

00:29:20.791 --> 00:29:22.025 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah?
DAVE: And he had a rash.

00:29:22.025 --> 00:29:23.828 line:0%
So I took that flea collar off.

00:29:23.828 --> 00:29:24.862 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, okay.

00:29:24.862 --> 00:29:25.896 line:0%
DAVE: And a little while later,

00:29:25.896 --> 00:29:27.132 line:0%
I noticed then
he had a little bump.

00:29:27.132 --> 00:29:28.332 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay.

00:29:28.332 --> 00:29:30.434 line:0%
DAVE: And now it's getting
quite a bit bigger.

00:29:30.434 --> 00:29:33.371 line:0%
Call me a hypochondriac
for my dog, I don't know, but.

00:29:33.371 --> 00:29:35.072 line:0%
MICHELLE: No,
it's understandable.

00:29:35.072 --> 00:29:37.075 line:0%
Understandably,
Dave is really sensitive.

00:29:37.075 --> 00:29:39.477 line:0%
He's still not over
Homer number one.

00:29:39.477 --> 00:29:41.112 line:0%
Um, he got this
wonderful new puppy,

00:29:41.112 --> 00:29:44.115 line:0%
and then suddenly he finds
a growth, a mass on him

00:29:44.115 --> 00:29:45.583 line:0%
that's growing quickly.

00:29:45.583 --> 00:29:47.418 line:0%
DAVE: Just hopefully,
it don't happen to him.

00:29:47.418 --> 00:29:48.886 line:0%
MICHELLE: No.

00:29:48.886 --> 00:29:50.488 line:0%
He's gonna want this
dealt with quickly

00:29:50.488 --> 00:29:52.123 line:0%
and we're gonna help him.

00:29:52.123 --> 00:29:54.124 line:0%
Oh, you're so wiggly.

00:29:54.124 --> 00:29:56.193 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But before Michelle
 tends to Homer's lump...</i>

00:29:56.193 --> 00:29:57.862 line:0%
MICHELLE: He's got all
his big boy teeth.

00:29:57.862 --> 00:29:59.297 line:0%
Oh, thank you!

00:29:59.297 --> 00:30:01.165 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: She'll check out
 his other puppy parts.</i>

00:30:01.165 --> 00:30:02.500 line:0%
MICHELLE: Coat is awesome.

00:30:02.500 --> 00:30:04.001 line:0%
So I'm getting Homer
on the table.

00:30:04.001 --> 00:30:05.569 line:0%
I'm trying to get him settled.

00:30:05.569 --> 00:30:09.073 line:0%
He's just so excited and playful
and wants to give kisses,

00:30:09.073 --> 00:30:12.977 line:0%
has these long, soft ears,
beautiful brown eyes.

00:30:12.977 --> 00:30:14.278 line:0%
He's just a sweetheart.

00:30:14.278 --> 00:30:16.947 line:0%
So no other lumps or bumps
anywhere, right?

00:30:16.947 --> 00:30:19.249 line:0%
DAVE: No, I haven't noticed
any during the daily pettings.

00:30:19.249 --> 00:30:21.452 line:0%
MICHELLE: Wait, don't go yet.

00:30:21.452 --> 00:30:22.953 line:0%
He's in my treat pocket.

00:30:22.953 --> 00:30:25.156 line:0%
DAVE: Yeah, he's crazy that way.

00:30:25.156 --> 00:30:28.525 line:0%
MICHELLE: He's got two in
the pocket as they should be.

00:30:28.525 --> 00:30:31.062 line:0%
All his squishy parts feel good.

00:30:31.062 --> 00:30:33.130 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Homer passes
 his physical.</i>

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:36.334 line:0%
<i> Now, it's time to inspect
 his suspicious mass.</i>

00:30:38.402 --> 00:30:39.970 line:0%
MICHELLE: Maybe we can get him
to lay down.

00:30:39.970 --> 00:30:41.005 line:0%
DAVE: Come on, Homer.

00:30:41.005 --> 00:30:42.273 line:0%
MICHELLE: That's a good boy.

00:30:42.273 --> 00:30:43.608 line:0%
Belly rub.

00:30:43.608 --> 00:30:47.011 line:0%
The growth on Homer's neck,
it could be a little cyst,

00:30:47.011 --> 00:30:50.080 line:0%
it could be a small wart,

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:52.383 line:0%
but also could be something
more insidious.

00:30:52.383 --> 00:30:54.184 line:0%
Good boy.

00:30:54.184 --> 00:30:55.419 line:0%
There we go, okay.

00:30:55.419 --> 00:30:56.487 line:0%
DAVE: Yeah, good boy.

00:30:56.487 --> 00:30:58.989 line:0%
MICHELLE: Almost looks pustuly.

00:30:58.989 --> 00:31:00.991 line:0%
How about I poke it, put it
on a slide, we'll have a look?

00:31:00.991 --> 00:31:02.026 line:0%
DAVE: Yeah.

00:31:02.026 --> 00:31:03.227 line:0%
MICHELLE: So the first thing
I want to do

00:31:03.227 --> 00:31:04.929 line:0%
is actually just
aspirate this mass.

00:31:04.929 --> 00:31:08.999 line:0%
I want to take some cells, and
look at it under the microscope.

00:31:08.999 --> 00:31:10.635 line:0%
Give him a distracting
little pet there.

00:31:10.635 --> 00:31:13.370 line:0%
DAVE: Homer, Homer, Homer.

00:31:13.370 --> 00:31:14.405 line:0%
Homer.

00:31:14.405 --> 00:31:16.006 line:0%
MICHELLE: Hold still, Homer.

00:31:16.006 --> 00:31:18.209 line:0%
So, yeah, he can take a break.

00:31:18.209 --> 00:31:19.477 line:0%
DAVE: Homer needs
a little break.

00:31:19.477 --> 00:31:21.512 line:0%
MICHELLE: I really just hope
we don't see any sign

00:31:21.512 --> 00:31:24.548 line:0%
of, you know, a really nasty
cancer cell.

00:31:24.548 --> 00:31:27.618 line:0%
There are several nasty types
of tumors this could be,

00:31:27.618 --> 00:31:29.553 line:0%
and Dave does not need
that right now.

00:31:29.553 --> 00:31:31.455 line:0%
I'm gonna have a peek at this.

00:31:31.455 --> 00:31:34.058 line:0%
(music)

00:31:34.058 --> 00:31:39.463 line:0%
(music)

00:31:39.463 --> 00:31:41.498 line:0%
TRACY: It's definitely not just
a little infected pustule.

00:31:41.498 --> 00:31:43.968 line:0%
MICHELLE: No, it's,
it's definitely a tumor.

00:31:50.274 --> 00:31:51.609 line:0%
DAVE: You want to play, huh?
You want to play?

00:31:51.609 --> 00:31:53.243 line:0%
Homer, come on, play.

00:31:53.243 --> 00:31:55.212 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Dave Stickler
 recently lost a young dog</i>

00:31:55.212 --> 00:31:56.714 line:0%
<i> to cancer.</i>

00:31:56.714 --> 00:32:01.452 line:0%
<i> Now, his new puppy has
 a suspicious lump on his neck.</i>

00:32:01.452 --> 00:32:03.721 line:0%
<i> Michelle's not sure what it is,</i>

00:32:03.721 --> 00:32:06.457 line:0%
<i> but a look under the microscope
 yields clues.</i>

00:32:06.457 --> 00:32:08.059 line:0%
TRACY: That's definitely not
just a little infected pustule.

00:32:08.059 --> 00:32:09.093 line:0%
MICHELLE: No.

00:32:09.093 --> 00:32:10.361 line:0%
After looking at the slide,

00:32:10.361 --> 00:32:13.230 line:0%
it looks pretty clear
that it's not just a cyst.

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:15.199 line:0%
Okay, I'm gonna chat with him.

00:32:16.633 --> 00:32:21.506 line:0%
Okay, so, bad news is it's not
just a little abscess,

00:32:21.506 --> 00:32:22.907 line:0%
it is a tumor.

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:28.579 line:0%
The good news is it looks more
like a type of tumor

00:32:28.579 --> 00:32:32.083 line:0%
that actually might go away
on its own in a few months.

00:32:35.185 --> 00:32:36.688 line:0%
It sort of looks like
a type of tumor

00:32:36.688 --> 00:32:40.358 line:0%
that's common on young dogs
called histiocytoma.

00:32:40.358 --> 00:32:42.493 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Histiocytomas
 are benign.</i>

00:32:42.493 --> 00:32:46.196 line:0%
<i> If that's what he has,
 then Homer's home free.</i>

00:32:46.196 --> 00:32:48.466 line:0%
<i> But diagnosing tumors
 through the microscope</i>

00:32:48.466 --> 00:32:49.733 line:0%
<i> is never certain.</i>

00:32:49.733 --> 00:32:51.435 line:0%
MICHELLE: You know,
I'm not like 100% sure

00:32:51.435 --> 00:32:52.670 line:0%
just looking at a slide.

00:32:52.670 --> 00:32:54.438 line:0%
We can leave it and watch it

00:32:54.438 --> 00:32:56.273 line:0%
and see if it starts to go away
on its own,

00:32:56.273 --> 00:32:58.642 line:0%
which they often do in young
dogs, but we wouldn't know.

00:32:58.642 --> 00:33:00.144 line:0%
Or we can remove it right now,

00:33:00.144 --> 00:33:01.779 line:0%
and I'm totally fine with just
getting it out of there,

00:33:01.779 --> 00:33:03.214 line:0%
'cause I know the history.

00:33:03.214 --> 00:33:04.348 line:0%
DAVE: You're fine with it?
MICHELLE: Yeah, yeah.

00:33:04.348 --> 00:33:06.083 line:0%
DAVE: Then so am I.
MICHELLE: Yeah, okay.

00:33:06.083 --> 00:33:07.785 line:0%
MICHELLE: I'm not entirely sure
what it is.

00:33:07.785 --> 00:33:09.520 line:0%
It's growing quickly.

00:33:09.520 --> 00:33:12.323 line:0%
The best thing we can do right
now is send it off to the lab

00:33:12.323 --> 00:33:13.490 line:0%
and hear back what it is.

00:33:13.490 --> 00:33:14.525 line:0%
Awesome.

00:33:14.525 --> 00:33:16.026 line:0%
DAVE: First surgery, boy.

00:33:16.026 --> 00:33:18.095 line:0%
MICHELLE: Don't worry,
we'll contact you soon. Okay.

00:33:18.095 --> 00:33:19.229 line:0%
(dog whimpering)
DAVE: Alright.

00:33:19.229 --> 00:33:21.365 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, Homer,
do you hear Dad leaving?

00:33:21.365 --> 00:33:22.666 line:0%
We want to get started quickly.

00:33:22.666 --> 00:33:25.502 line:0%
You know, we have to sedate
Homer, do a local.

00:33:25.502 --> 00:33:28.272 line:0%
You're so brave for a baby.

00:33:28.272 --> 00:33:31.241 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: A cocktail of drugs
 will relieve Homer's anxiety</i>

00:33:31.241 --> 00:33:34.111 line:0%
<i> and put him to sleep.</i>

00:33:34.111 --> 00:33:36.080 line:0%
MICHELLE: It's okay, Homer,
you can just chillax.

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:37.214 line:0%
TRACY: I'll just put
the lights out.

00:33:37.214 --> 00:33:38.383 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah.
(laughs)

00:33:42.686 --> 00:33:45.255 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: If Homer's lump
 turns out to be cancer,</i>

00:33:45.255 --> 00:33:48.126 line:0%
<i> he could face a grueling fight
 for his young life.</i>

00:33:50.194 --> 00:33:53.965 line:0%
MICHELLE: It's hard to cut
on him, his skin is so floppy.

00:33:57.668 --> 00:33:58.836 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 In under two minutes...</i>

00:33:58.836 --> 00:34:00.071 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay.

00:34:00.071 --> 00:34:01.439 line:0%
There's our lovely friend.

00:34:01.439 --> 00:34:03.507 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Homer's tumor-free.</i>

00:34:03.507 --> 00:34:04.542 line:0%
MICHELLE: Doesn't look like

00:34:04.542 --> 00:34:06.577 line:0%
there's a bunch of roots
going deep.

00:34:06.577 --> 00:34:08.312 line:0%
Just close that up.

00:34:08.312 --> 00:34:10.081 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Clean tissue
 is promising,</i>

00:34:10.081 --> 00:34:13.651 line:0%
<i> but only a pathologist can tell
 if the mass is cancerous.</i>

00:34:13.651 --> 00:34:16.086 line:0%
TRACY: Alrighty,
ready for the lab.

00:34:16.086 --> 00:34:18.322 line:0%
MICHELLE: Hi, Homer,
time to get up.

00:34:18.322 --> 00:34:20.591 line:0%
Homer's waking up,
so all is looking good.

00:34:20.591 --> 00:34:21.625 line:0%
He's up and ready.

00:34:21.625 --> 00:34:22.659 line:0%
DAVE: Okay.

00:34:22.659 --> 00:34:23.694 line:0%
MICHELLE: So, he, it went great,

00:34:23.694 --> 00:34:26.163 line:0%
yeah, so the anesthesia
was perfect.

00:34:26.163 --> 00:34:29.232 line:0%
No problems at all,
so, I just removed it.

00:34:29.232 --> 00:34:30.668 line:0%
Now that I've reassured Dave,

00:34:30.668 --> 00:34:33.271 line:0%
it's, you know, and tried
to get him not to worry,

00:34:33.271 --> 00:34:35.338 line:0%
it's trying to keep
my own worry down,

00:34:35.338 --> 00:34:37.341 line:0%
kind of keep my worry hidden.

00:34:37.341 --> 00:34:38.409 line:0%
Look who's here!

00:34:38.409 --> 00:34:39.543 line:0%
DAVE: Hi, Homer.

00:34:39.543 --> 00:34:41.545 line:0%
Hey, little buddy.

00:34:41.545 --> 00:34:43.681 line:0%
Oh, yeah, you took a little nap.

00:34:43.681 --> 00:34:46.517 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Lab results should
 come back within a week.</i>

00:34:46.517 --> 00:34:48.752 line:0%
MICHELLE: There is nothing worse
than getting news

00:34:48.752 --> 00:34:50.320 line:0%
that you don't like to deliver

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:51.755 line:0%
and then having to call someone

00:34:51.755 --> 00:34:55.459 line:0%
especially after
they've lost one dog.

00:34:55.459 --> 00:34:58.562 line:0%
DAVE: Gotta keep
our boy healthy.

00:34:58.562 --> 00:35:01.198 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: With the young
 hound's future unclear,</i>

00:35:01.198 --> 00:35:04.602 line:0%
<i> the only thing left
 to do is wait.</i>

00:35:04.602 --> 00:35:06.904 line:0%
MICHELLE: Good news or bad,
as soon as I find out,

00:35:06.904 --> 00:35:09.540 line:0%
I have to let Dave know,
either way.

00:35:09.540 --> 00:35:10.875 line:0%
DAVE: Come on, Homer.

00:35:10.875 --> 00:35:12.710 line:0%
(whistles)

00:35:12.710 --> 00:35:14.445 line:0%
Good boy.

00:35:17.414 --> 00:35:20.918 line:0%
(music)

00:35:20.918 --> 00:35:22.586 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Michelle gets
 her mind off</i>

00:35:22.586 --> 00:35:27.257 line:0%
<i> Homer's pending test results
 with a trip to the country.</i>

00:35:27.257 --> 00:35:30.594 line:0%
<i> She's headed to Honey Hill Farm
 where a flock of Shetland sheep</i>

00:35:30.594 --> 00:35:32.796 line:0%
<i> is expecting a new
 batch of babies.</i>

00:35:32.796 --> 00:35:35.332 line:0%
MICHELLE: So I'm here today
to visit Mike and Penne

00:35:35.332 --> 00:35:36.800 line:0%
and their sheep farm.

00:35:36.800 --> 00:35:38.502 line:0%
They have 40 or 50 head

00:35:38.502 --> 00:35:42.339 line:0%
and about 11 pregnant females
that are getting ready to lamb.

00:35:42.339 --> 00:35:43.841 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Hailing from
 the Scottish Highlands,</i>

00:35:43.841 --> 00:35:47.210 line:0%
<i> Shetland sheep are prized
 for their hardy constitutions</i>

00:35:47.210 --> 00:35:49.446 line:0%
<i> and luxurious fleece.</i>

00:35:49.446 --> 00:35:51.816 line:0%
MIKE: We've got a little bit
of enterprise involved

00:35:51.816 --> 00:35:54.918 line:0%
with processing
and selling wool.

00:35:54.918 --> 00:36:00.324 line:0%
We've bonded very nicely
with the sheep.

00:36:00.324 --> 00:36:03.260 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Now, one of their
 mamas-to-be is showing signs</i>

00:36:03.260 --> 00:36:05.329 line:0%
<i> that she may be unwell.</i>

00:36:05.329 --> 00:36:06.363 line:0%
MICHELLE: Hello.

00:36:06.363 --> 00:36:07.764 line:0%
PENNE: Hi.
MIKE: Good morning.

00:36:07.764 --> 00:36:10.668 line:0%
MICHELLE: So you have some sheep
for us to look at, I heard?

00:36:10.668 --> 00:36:12.869 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Last year,
 a young ewe named Kalina</i>

00:36:12.869 --> 00:36:16.540 line:0%
<i> had a grueling labor while
 trying to give birth to twins.</i>

00:36:16.540 --> 00:36:19.810 line:0%
PENNE: They were both in the
vaginal canal at the same time.

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:21.645 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, okay.
PENNE: So it was...

00:36:21.645 --> 00:36:22.846 line:0%
MICHELLE: That doesn't work,

00:36:22.846 --> 00:36:24.548 line:0%
it's a one at a time
kind of a situation there.

00:36:24.548 --> 00:36:25.816 line:0%
PENNE: Yeah, right, yeah.

00:36:25.816 --> 00:36:27.985 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Both her lambs
 died during delivery.</i>

00:36:27.985 --> 00:36:30.320 line:0%
MICHELLE: We don't want to
stress her too much for sure.

00:36:30.320 --> 00:36:31.689 line:0%
PENNE: It's okay, mom.

00:36:31.689 --> 00:36:33.958 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Now, Kalina is
 expecting again,</i>

00:36:33.958 --> 00:36:36.994 line:0%
<i> and her physical condition
 doesn't bode well.</i>

00:36:36.994 --> 00:36:38.628 line:0%
PENNE: This year, she's fat.

00:36:38.628 --> 00:36:40.797 line:0%
MICHELLE:
Oh, yeah, she's big. Yeah.

00:36:40.797 --> 00:36:41.999 line:0%
PENNE: She is big.

00:36:41.999 --> 00:36:44.668 line:0%
I'm concerned about her.

00:36:44.668 --> 00:36:48.206 line:0%
MICHELLE: She's looking huge,
much bigger than the other ones.

00:36:51.008 --> 00:36:52.243 line:0%
PENNE: Okay.

00:36:52.243 --> 00:36:53.978 line:0%
Okay, girl, okay, alright.

00:36:53.978 --> 00:36:55.479 line:0%
MICHELLE: When they get
really fat,

00:36:55.479 --> 00:36:57.948 line:0%
it sets them up for some
problems with their metabolism.

00:36:57.948 --> 00:37:00.351 line:0%
But you know, she's at risk
for the pregnancy toxemia,

00:37:00.351 --> 00:37:01.485 line:0%
like, when she's this big.

00:37:01.485 --> 00:37:02.619 line:0%
PENNE: Yeah.

00:37:02.619 --> 00:37:04.355 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Pregnancy toxemia
 is a disorder</i>

00:37:04.355 --> 00:37:06.958 line:0%
<i> caused when the ewe doesn't
 get enough to eat.</i>

00:37:06.958 --> 00:37:08.659 line:0%
PENNE: It's alright,
it's alright, it's alright.

00:37:08.659 --> 00:37:10.961 line:0%
Oh, my gosh,
how much does she weigh?

00:37:10.961 --> 00:37:13.463 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Overweight ewes
 are most at risk</i>

00:37:13.463 --> 00:37:15.966 line:0%
<i> because there's little room
 for the stomach to expand</i>

00:37:15.966 --> 00:37:17.635 line:0%
<i> and take in nourishment.</i>

00:37:17.635 --> 00:37:20.671 line:0%
MICHELLE: You know, it's a big
cascade of things going wrong.

00:37:20.671 --> 00:37:22.573 line:0%
The mom's not getting
the glucose she needs,

00:37:22.573 --> 00:37:24.975 line:0%
and she needs glucose
to run her brain.

00:37:24.975 --> 00:37:26.810 line:0%
Let's get the glucometer ready.

00:37:26.810 --> 00:37:28.845 line:0%
It'll kill the lambs,
it'll kill her,

00:37:28.845 --> 00:37:30.581 line:0%
and it can be fatal like that.

00:37:30.581 --> 00:37:31.615 line:0%
So I think we should take
a quick blood sample,

00:37:31.615 --> 00:37:32.649 line:0%
we can check her glucose level.

00:37:32.649 --> 00:37:33.684 line:0%
PENNE: Okay.

00:37:33.684 --> 00:37:35.052 line:0%
MICHELLE: And see
where she's at.

00:37:35.052 --> 00:37:37.754 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: If Kalina's
 blood sugar has fallen too low,</i>

00:37:37.754 --> 00:37:40.590 line:0%
<i> Michelle may have to perform
 an emergency C-section</i>

00:37:40.590 --> 00:37:41.992 line:0%
<i> to deliver her lambs.</i>

00:37:41.992 --> 00:37:43.760 line:0%
PENNE: It's alright, girl.
It's alright, it's alright.

00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:45.796 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But to get
 the blood sample they need,</i>

00:37:45.796 --> 00:37:47.665 line:0%
<i> Kalina must be restrained,</i>

00:37:47.665 --> 00:37:49.967 line:0%
<i> and her nervous energy
 is spreading.</i>

00:37:49.967 --> 00:37:52.569 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay, everybody. Okay,
everybody, slow it down. Shh.

00:37:52.569 --> 00:37:53.670 line:0%
PENNE: I can't let go
of her head.

00:37:53.670 --> 00:37:54.972 line:0%
MIKE: I know.
PENNE: Shh, shh, shh, shh.

00:37:54.972 --> 00:37:56.073 line:0%
MICHELLE: It's okay.

00:37:56.073 --> 00:37:58.742 line:0%
PENNE: Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh.
Here I go.

00:37:58.742 --> 00:37:59.777 line:0%
MICHELLE: Are you okay?

00:37:59.777 --> 00:38:01.879 line:0%
PENNE: I'm okay.

00:38:01.879 --> 00:38:03.580 line:0%
Okay, I got her.

00:38:03.580 --> 00:38:04.882 line:0%
MICHELLE: Kalina's
really worked up.

00:38:04.882 --> 00:38:06.817 line:0%
Sheep are trampling
over everyone.

00:38:06.817 --> 00:38:08.352 line:0%
Oh, you poor big girl.

00:38:08.352 --> 00:38:09.586 line:0%
Oh, my lordy.

00:38:09.586 --> 00:38:11.889 line:0%
It's getting a little
too chaotic here

00:38:11.889 --> 00:38:14.658 line:0%
in this small space,
so we need to slow it down,

00:38:14.658 --> 00:38:18.395 line:0%
just focus on Kalina, and try
and get this sample quickly.

00:38:18.395 --> 00:38:19.530 line:0%
Alright, alright, alright.

00:38:19.530 --> 00:38:20.898 line:0%
TRACY: Easy, easy, easy.

00:38:20.898 --> 00:38:22.499 line:0%
MICHELLE: It's okay.

00:38:22.499 --> 00:38:24.501 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Michelle only needs
 one drop of blood</i>

00:38:24.501 --> 00:38:26.971 line:0%
<i> for the glucose test.</i>

00:38:26.971 --> 00:38:29.406 line:0%
PENNE: Oh, that's alright.

00:38:29.406 --> 00:38:30.974 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Any second,
 the Sentas will know</i>

00:38:30.974 --> 00:38:35.445 line:0%
<i> if the young mother and her
 unborn lambs are going toxic.</i>

00:38:35.445 --> 00:38:36.480 line:0%
PENNE: It's okay.

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:37.615 line:0%
(beeping)

00:38:42.986 --> 00:38:45.689 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay, okay,
okay, okay.

00:38:45.689 --> 00:38:48.692 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: A pregnant ewe is
 shockingly overweight,</i>

00:38:48.692 --> 00:38:52.529 line:0%
<i> putting her at risk
 for a deadly condition.</i>

00:38:52.529 --> 00:38:54.832 line:0%
MICHELLE: Good job, sweetheart.
I'm so sorry.

00:38:54.832 --> 00:38:55.966 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: A blood test
 will tell</i>

00:38:55.966 --> 00:38:59.136 line:0%
<i> if she suffers from
 pregnancy toxemia.</i>

00:38:59.136 --> 00:39:01.305 line:0%
PENNE: Oh, it's alright.

00:39:04.074 --> 00:39:05.909 line:0%
It's okay.

00:39:05.909 --> 00:39:08.145 line:0%
(beeping)

00:39:08.145 --> 00:39:09.713 line:0%
TRACY: 84.

00:39:09.713 --> 00:39:12.950 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oh, good. Okay.
So she's, she's okay. Awesome.

00:39:12.950 --> 00:39:15.018 line:0%
So the test indicates
that pregnancy toxemia

00:39:15.018 --> 00:39:16.853 line:0%
is not an issue with Kalina.

00:39:16.853 --> 00:39:19.857 line:0%
Her glucose is normal, actually,
so that all seems great.

00:39:19.857 --> 00:39:21.458 line:0%
You want to hold that leg
or you want to do that?

00:39:21.458 --> 00:39:22.659 line:0%
PENNE: Sure.
MICHELLE: Okay, yeah.

00:39:22.659 --> 00:39:24.762 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: All that's left is
 a physical exam</i>

00:39:24.762 --> 00:39:29.399 line:0%
<i> to make sure this year,
 her babies survive delivery.</i>

00:39:29.399 --> 00:39:30.434 line:0%
MICHELLE: Poor lovey.

00:39:30.434 --> 00:39:31.735 line:0%
I want to get in there,

00:39:31.735 --> 00:39:33.470 line:0%
I want to feel if there's a lamb
starting to come out,

00:39:33.470 --> 00:39:36.940 line:0%
just kind of see at what stage
in labor, if any, she's at.

00:39:36.940 --> 00:39:40.477 line:0%
It looks pretty swollen,
it does, around there.

00:39:40.477 --> 00:39:44.014 line:0%
So I'm just making sure that
there's not a lamb that's stuck,

00:39:44.014 --> 00:39:46.883 line:0%
that there's not again
two faces coming at once,

00:39:46.883 --> 00:39:49.620 line:0%
that everything feels normal.

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:50.754 line:0%
Hold on, my love.
I know, I know.

00:39:50.754 --> 00:39:52.556 line:0%
I'm not going in any further.

00:39:52.556 --> 00:39:53.957 line:0%
TRACY: It's okay, baby.

00:39:53.957 --> 00:39:55.826 line:0%
MICHELLE: Have you noticed her
pushing at all or anything?

00:39:55.826 --> 00:39:56.827 line:0%
MIKE: No.
MICHELLE: Okay.

00:39:56.827 --> 00:39:57.861 line:0%
MIKE: No, just...

00:39:57.861 --> 00:39:59.162 line:0%
MICHELLE: There's feet
right there.

00:39:59.162 --> 00:40:02.065 line:0%
The cervix is pretty much open
and the feet are there,

00:40:02.065 --> 00:40:04.134 line:0%
or they're coming.

00:40:04.134 --> 00:40:06.102 line:0%
As I'm in there palpating
and feeling,

00:40:06.102 --> 00:40:08.672 line:0%
I can feel movements
of the fetuses in there,

00:40:08.672 --> 00:40:10.640 line:0%
so that's, that's good news.

00:40:10.640 --> 00:40:11.942 line:0%
They're heading
in the right direction

00:40:11.942 --> 00:40:14.044 line:0%
and everybody feels
happy and healthy.

00:40:14.044 --> 00:40:15.545 line:0%
And she's starting to dilate.

00:40:15.545 --> 00:40:16.647 line:0%
So I think she's just,

00:40:16.647 --> 00:40:18.081 line:0%
she's just starting
is what's going on.

00:40:18.081 --> 00:40:19.450 line:0%
PENNE: Okay.

00:40:19.450 --> 00:40:20.750 line:0%
MICHELLE: And I gave a little
stretch as well,

00:40:20.750 --> 00:40:22.786 line:0%
and that's just gonna stimulate
her to have the lambs,

00:40:22.786 --> 00:40:25.923 line:0%
and, really,
that's what she needs.

00:40:25.923 --> 00:40:28.158 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: As long as Kalina
 remains pregnant,</i>

00:40:28.158 --> 00:40:30.761 line:0%
<i> she runs the risk
 of developing toxemia.</i>

00:40:30.761 --> 00:40:32.562 line:0%
PENNE: Okay, it's alright,
it's alright.

00:40:32.562 --> 00:40:34.031 line:0%
MICHELLE: She needs to,
to get the lambs out

00:40:34.031 --> 00:40:35.632 line:0%
or there's gonna be trouble.

00:40:35.632 --> 00:40:37.467 line:0%
If she doesn't lamb
in the next couple days,

00:40:37.467 --> 00:40:40.838 line:0%
then we should, you know,
induce her and get her moving.

00:40:40.838 --> 00:40:41.906 line:0%
PENNE: Okay.
MICHELLE: Keep me updated.

00:40:41.906 --> 00:40:42.940 line:0%
PENNE: I will.

00:40:42.940 --> 00:40:44.508 line:0%
MICHELLE: I'll have
my phone going,

00:40:44.508 --> 00:40:46.142 line:0%
I'll be ready for the text
that they're on their way out.

00:40:46.142 --> 00:40:47.511 line:0%
PENNE: We'll definitely
let you know.

00:40:47.511 --> 00:40:48.879 line:0%
MICHELLE: Okay.

00:40:56.152 --> 00:40:58.956 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: As a new day shines
 on Haines, Alaska,</i>

00:40:58.956 --> 00:41:02.058 line:0%
<i> Michelle is back at the
 American Bald Eagle Foundation,</i>

00:41:02.058 --> 00:41:04.694 line:0%
<i> where important news
 has just come in.</i>

00:41:04.694 --> 00:41:07.798 line:0%
DAVE: Hey, little buddy.
Oh, my buddy.

00:41:07.798 --> 00:41:09.733 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yes.

00:41:09.733 --> 00:41:11.034 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: A few days ago,</i>

00:41:11.034 --> 00:41:13.503 line:0%
<i> Michelle removed a suspicious
 tumor from Homer,</i>

00:41:13.503 --> 00:41:16.573 line:0%
<i> a five-month-old
 coonhound puppy.</i>

00:41:16.573 --> 00:41:18.675 line:0%
<i> Now, she's about
 to tell his owner</i>

00:41:18.675 --> 00:41:21.612 line:0%
<i> whether the mass is
 cancerous or benign.</i>

00:41:21.612 --> 00:41:23.113 line:0%
(phone ringing)

00:41:23.113 --> 00:41:26.583 line:0%
DAVE: Hello? My phone says
it's Dr. Oakley.

00:41:26.583 --> 00:41:28.118 line:0%
MICHELLE: Your phone is right.

00:41:28.118 --> 00:41:30.253 line:0%
My phone says it's Mr. Stickler.

00:41:30.253 --> 00:41:31.288 line:0%
(laughs)

00:41:31.288 --> 00:41:32.923 line:0%
DAVE: News on Homer?

00:41:32.923 --> 00:41:35.025 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah, I do, so I just
wanted to talk to you about it.

00:41:35.025 --> 00:41:36.926 line:0%
DAVE: And?

00:41:36.926 --> 00:41:41.031 line:0%
MICHELLE: And so, it is
a benign tumor.

00:41:41.031 --> 00:41:42.299 line:0%
It's that histiocytoma,

00:41:42.299 --> 00:41:44.667 line:0%
the one I told you about
that young dogs tend to get.

00:41:44.667 --> 00:41:47.737 line:0%
It's, it's the best news
we were hoping for.

00:41:47.737 --> 00:41:48.772 line:0%
DAVE: Well, good.

00:41:48.772 --> 00:41:50.274 line:0%
I'm, I'm glad of that,

00:41:50.274 --> 00:41:52.676 line:0%
but I've not had that
good of luck here lately.

00:41:52.676 --> 00:41:53.944 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yeah.

00:41:53.944 --> 00:41:56.913 line:0%
It feels great to have good news
to give Dave today.

00:41:56.913 --> 00:41:58.715 line:0%
I mean, it was not that long ago

00:41:58.715 --> 00:42:01.018 line:0%
that I had to give him
the worst news.

00:42:01.018 --> 00:42:06.123 line:0%
So, I'm just so relieved and I'm
just excited to make his day,

00:42:06.123 --> 00:42:08.125 line:0%
and we're both smiling
this time.

00:42:08.125 --> 00:42:10.727 line:0%
Just watch that spot, make sure
it continues to heal up nicely,

00:42:10.727 --> 00:42:12.262 line:0%
but there shouldn't be
any other issues

00:42:12.262 --> 00:42:13.864 line:0%
or any other reasons
to bring him in.

00:42:13.864 --> 00:42:15.632 line:0%
DAVE: Okay, right on.

00:42:15.632 --> 00:42:17.768 line:0%
Thank you so much, Michelle,
for getting back to me.

00:42:17.768 --> 00:42:18.902 line:0%
MICHELLE: Hey, no problem.

00:42:18.902 --> 00:42:21.304 line:0%
DAVE: Okay, bye-bye.
MICHELLE: Okay, bye-bye.

00:42:21.304 --> 00:42:24.908 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yay!
I love giving good news.

00:42:24.908 --> 00:42:29.346 line:0%
(music)

00:42:29.346 --> 00:42:31.782 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: It's been an
 exhausting time for Michelle,</i>

00:42:31.782 --> 00:42:33.950 line:0%
<i> traveling cross-country
 and back again,</i>

00:42:33.950 --> 00:42:39.022 line:0%
<i> but now, she's finally made it
 back to home sweet Yukon.</i>

00:42:39.022 --> 00:42:42.593 line:0%
<i> Now, she can do something
 she rarely gets to do.</i>

00:42:42.593 --> 00:42:44.961 line:0%
MICHELLE: Oooh!

00:42:44.961 --> 00:42:47.230 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 Relax with her family.</i>

00:42:47.230 --> 00:42:48.898 line:0%
MICHELLE: Smoothies?
MAYA: Yeah, go for it.

00:42:48.898 --> 00:42:50.233 line:0%
SHANE: Yeah, you want this one?

00:42:50.233 --> 00:42:52.269 line:0%
MICHELLE: What's going on?
You guys don't want them?

00:42:52.269 --> 00:42:53.737 line:0%
It's crazy weeks like this

00:42:53.737 --> 00:42:57.775 line:0%
that make me really appreciate
just being home.

00:42:57.775 --> 00:42:59.309 line:0%
I don't think we...

00:42:59.309 --> 00:43:01.177 line:0%
Maybe that needs
a bit more blending.

00:43:01.177 --> 00:43:02.345 line:0%
I love kind of doing nothing,

00:43:02.345 --> 00:43:04.148 line:0%
just hanging out
with Shane and the kids.

00:43:04.148 --> 00:43:07.851 line:0%
Oh, my gosh,
you guys, check this out.

00:43:07.851 --> 00:43:10.653 line:0%
This is that sheep I was
telling you about, Kalina,

00:43:10.653 --> 00:43:14.825 line:0%
that was, like, huge and having
some issues with her babies.

00:43:14.825 --> 00:43:16.994 line:0%
Look it, they,
she just had them.

00:43:16.994 --> 00:43:19.095 line:0%
MAYA: Oh, my gosh.
MICHELLE: Yay!

00:43:19.095 --> 00:43:20.864 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 After a difficult pregnancy,</i>

00:43:20.864 --> 00:43:23.934 line:0%
<i> Kalina had her lambs
 on Mother's Day.</i>

00:43:23.934 --> 00:43:24.968 line:0%
MICHELLE: We're more worried

00:43:24.968 --> 00:43:26.236 line:0%
that the babies
were gonna be stuck.

00:43:26.236 --> 00:43:27.303 line:0%
We were watching her
really closely,

00:43:27.303 --> 00:43:29.339 line:0%
but she did it all by herself.

00:43:29.339 --> 00:43:30.974 line:0%
MICHELLE: Yay!
MAYA: Oh, good for her!

00:43:30.974 --> 00:43:32.275 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Normal birth weight
 for a lamb</i>

00:43:32.275 --> 00:43:34.811 line:0%
<i> is between
 four and seven pounds,</i>

00:43:34.811 --> 00:43:36.679 line:0%
<i> but Kalina's twins clocked in</i>

00:43:36.679 --> 00:43:39.316 line:0%
<i> at seven and eight pounds
 apiece.</i>

00:43:39.316 --> 00:43:42.085 line:0%
<i> The Sentas named them
 Kali and Kai.</i>

00:43:42.085 --> 00:43:43.119 line:0%
MICHELLE: So Kalina looks great.

00:43:43.119 --> 00:43:44.888 line:0%
She gave birth,
had no trouble there,

00:43:44.888 --> 00:43:47.190 line:0%
she has two healthy lambs,
and that's really good.

00:43:47.190 --> 00:43:49.326 line:0%
As long as the lambs
are nursing quite well

00:43:49.326 --> 00:43:51.761 line:0%
and she keeps eating good,
she's gonna be fine.

00:43:51.761 --> 00:43:52.997 line:0%
That one kind of looks like me.

00:43:52.997 --> 00:43:55.398 line:0%
MAYA: I really want that one.
Can we adopt one?

00:43:55.398 --> 00:43:57.267 line:0%
MICHELLE: No one's
adopting anything.

00:43:57.267 --> 00:43:59.936 line:0%
It gives me a good feeling
to wrap up such a good week

00:43:59.936 --> 00:44:01.238 line:0%
when everyone's doing well.

00:44:01.238 --> 00:44:02.405 line:0%
And now my worry is gone,

00:44:02.405 --> 00:44:04.808 line:0%
so I can just rest up
until the next call.

00:44:04.808 --> 00:44:08.646
Why don't we go out for
breakfast, how about that? Okay?

